## 🌐 The Complete Technology Threat Matrix
To view the true breadth of cybersecurity, attacks are mapped across the entire operational stack:
## 1. Business Logic & Workflow Attacks
These attacks do not use malicious code or special characters. Instead, they exploit flaws in how a programmer designed a business feature or transaction sequence. [6] 

* Race Conditions: Exploiting tiny delays in server processing speeds by sending multiple requests at the exact same millisecond (e.g., hitting a "withdraw $10" API 5 times simultaneously to trick a database into dispensing $50 from a $10 account balance). [7, 8] 
* Price Manipulation: Altering hidden shopping cart data fields from a positive integer to a negative value or a decimal to purchase high-value inventory items for pennies. [9] 

## 2. Client-Side Sinks & Browser Attacks (Beyond XSS)

* CSRF (Cross-Site Request Forgery): Tricking a logged-in user into visiting an external webpage that silently hosts an invisible auto-submitting form, forcing their browser to perform actions (like updating a password) on the target site without their consent. [10, 11, 12, 13] 
* Clickjacking: Layering an invisible, transparent iframe of a sensitive portal (like a "Delete Account" button) directly over a harmless-looking button (like a fake video player) to trick users into clicking options they cannot see. [14, 15, 16] 

## 3. API & Cloud Infrastructure Attacks

* BOLA / BFLA (Broken Object/Function Level Authorization): The modern, API-centric mutation of IDOR, where mobile apps or single-page apps pull unprotected JSON records straight from leaky backend route endpoints (e.g., /api/v1/get_invoice/9932). [17, 18, 19] 
* Cloud Metadata SSRF: Targeting applications hosted on AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud to force the server to curl the private internal cloud link http://169.254.169. This leaks highly sensitive cloud access keys and infrastructure tokens. [20, 21] 

## 4. Network & Denial of Service (DoS) Attacks

* DDoS (Distributed Denial of Service): Flooding a server's network bandwidth with millions of garbage data packets using a botnet (a coordinated network of infected consumer devices) to force the host computer offline. [22, 23, 24, 25, 26] 
* DNS Spoofing / Cache Poisoning: Altering the domain name routing maps on internet routers to seamlessly redirect legitimate web traffic away from real banking portals and straight onto an attacker's lookalike phishing page. [27, 28, 29, 30, 31] 

## 5. Social Engineering & The Human Layer

* Spear Phishing & Whaling: Ultra-targeted, custom-tailored social engineering campaigns that research an executive's specific professional background to trick them into executing malware attachments or authorizing wire transfers. [32, 33, 34] 
* Watering Hole Attacks: Compromising a trusted third-party website frequently visited by employees of a target organization to indirectly drop tracking malware or exploits straight onto their workstation computers. [35, 36, 37, 38] 

